{
 "gdd": "command-simulation-layer",
 "blocking": [
  "CR-15 (L142), States LOADING row (L166, also L148/L169) vs Save/Load SL-23 (2) (L286), T-8 step 7 (L444), F-8 t_epoch (L704), RESTORE_EPOCH_WRITE_MAX_MS knob (L966): [main review; confirmed by creative-director spot-check] Delegated ruling R1 has two incompatible implementations, one in each GDD. Kernel CR-15 says Save/Load calls request_restore after it 'has already incremented it and durably written the slot header'. L166 says LOADING is 'Atomic within one boundary service, it never spans a frame'. Save/Load SL-23 (2) says something different. The header-only rewrite is 'issued at the staging step', 'runs while the kernel is in LOADING', and the end-of-LOADING hand-off 'waits for its EPOCH_DURABLE report'. On failure, or after RESTORE_EPOCH_WRITE_MAX_MS (500 ms placeholder), 'play proceeds with the in-memory value'. As a result: (a) the CR-15 precondition is false; (b) the kernel never says its K-4 hand-off may block the main thread for up to 500 ms inside an 'atomic' LOADING; (c) on the timeout or failure path, R1's 'durable before the first RUNNING tick' does not hold. A kill then replays the same epoch, which is the repeat R1 was ruled to prevent. KRN-EPO-04 / AC-SL-142 cannot hold on that path. Ruling R1 itself is not reopened. Only its contradictory implementation is at issue."
 ],
 "disagreements": [
  "No specialists were run (lean confirming re-review), so there are no specialist-vs-specialist disagreements.",
  "[creative-director vs main review, severity only] I agree with every finding and with the single BLOCKING item. I would treat the SAVING-mid-loop accumulator defect as the most urgent RECOMMENDED item: it is a live correctness bug (a negative accumulator starves frames), not a documentation gap. It stays RECOMMENDED because the fix is a one-rule edit. The cross-GDD ctx-contract propagation does not block this GDD, but it should gate the implementation epic for any consumer system."
 ]
